<h1>Pneumatic Conveying Systems</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">A pneumatic conveying system moves dry bulk material (powders, granules, pellets, and flakes) through a sealed pipeline using air. A blower, compressor, or vacuum pump either pushes material from a pressurized feed point or pulls it toward a vacuum receiver. The enclosed line contains dust, protects product from contamination, and routes around walls, floors, and equipment, carrying material from silos, bulk bags, and hoppers to mixers, process equipment, and <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/">packaging lines</a>.</p>
<p>Two choices define every system: the conveying phase (dilute or dense) and the air source (pressure or vacuum). The phase is set by how fast the gas moves relative to the material's saltation velocity. The air source is set by whether the air mover sits at the start of the line (pressure) or the end (vacuum).</p>
<h2 id="phase-heading">Dilute Phase vs. Dense Phase (Lean Phase vs. Dense Phase)</h2>
<p>Dilute phase is also called lean phase. The dividing line is the <strong>saltation velocity</strong>: the gas velocity at which fully suspended particles in a horizontal line begin to drop out and settle on the bottom of the pipe. Dilute phase runs above it. Dense phase runs below it. The typical figures below come from Eric Maynard of Jenike &amp; Johanson, writing in AIChE's <a href="https://www.aiche.org/sites/default/files/cep/20221147.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Chemical Engineering Progress</a>. They are typical values, not hard limits.</p>
