<p>Actual rate and distance depend on material properties, pipe size, route (bends and vertical lift), and air supply. Maynard notes that vacuum systems are typically limited to less than 300 ft, while positive pressure systems can convey over long distances. Suppliers confirm feasibility with material testing. See the <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/">conveyor cost guide</a> for cost factors.</p>
<h2 id="modes-heading">How Each Mode Works</h2>
<h3>Dilute Phase Pressure</h3>
<p>A blower pushes air through the line, and a rotary airlock valve meters material from the hopper into the pressurized pipe while holding the air seal. Air velocity stays above the material's saltation velocity, the speed below which particles drop out of the airstream and settle in horizontal pipe. At the destination, a filter receiver or cyclone separates material from the air. Diverter valves let one source feed several destinations. Dilute phase pressure is the default for free-flowing materials that tolerate impact.</p>
<h3>Dilute Phase Vacuum</h3>
<p>A vacuum pump at the destination pulls air and material through the line. Material enters at open pickup points (a wand, a bag or drum station, a hopper) with no airlock at the source, and several pickup points can feed one receiver. Leaks draw air inward, so dust stays inside the system. Rate and distance are limited because the pressure difference a vacuum system can use never exceeds atmospheric pressure. Maynard puts the realistic limit for most industrial vacuum systems at about 20 in. Hg, and recommends dilute phase pressure when the route exceeds about 300 ft or the line pressure drop exceeds about 10 psi. See the <a href="/conveyor-types/vacuum-conveyors/">vacuum conveyor guide</a> for vacuum belt and tube systems.</p>
<h3>Dense Phase Pressure</h3>
<p>Material loads into a pressure vessel (transporter or blow pot), which is sealed and pressurized with compressed air. Material leaves the vessel in one of two dense phase modes. In plug flow, coarse, permeable, free-flowing solids such as plastic pellets, coffee beans, and seeds move as discrete plugs with air passing through them. In moving bed flow, fine powders that fluidize and hold air, such as cement and fine ash, slide along the pipe as a dense bed. Low velocity reduces particle breakage, blend segregation, and pipe and elbow wear. Pressure vessels, heavy-duty valves, flanged pipe, and a compressed air supply make dense phase cost more to install than dilute phase. Colin Barbeau of Hatch, writing in <a href="https://www.powderbulksolids.com/pneumatic-conveying/is-dilute-or-dense-phase-right-for-my-material-" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Powder &amp; Bulk Solids</a>, also describes a mixed or medium phase that runs near the saltation velocity and suits fluidizable materials.</p>
<h3>Dense Phase Vacuum</h3>
<p>High-vacuum pumps pull material at low velocity over short runs. It is the least common mode and appears mostly in pharmaceutical and specialty chemical plants that need gentle handling and full containment.</p>
<h2 id="air-heading">Blower vs. Air Compressor</h2>
<p>The air mover sets the pressure and airflow the system can deliver and drives most of its operating cost. An undersized air mover plugs lines. An oversized one wastes energy and raises velocity, which increases breakage and wear.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Positive displacement blowers:</strong> high airflow at low pressure. The standard air source for dilute phase pressure systems, usually dedicated to one conveying line.</li>
<li><strong>Vacuum pumps and exhausters:</strong> regenerative blowers, rotary lobe exhausters, rotary vane, or liquid ring pumps, chosen by required vacuum level and duty. They power dilute and dense phase vacuum systems.</li>
<li><strong>Air compressors:</strong> higher pressure at lower airflow. Needed for dense phase transporters and air-assist devices. Supply comes from a dedicated compressor or the plant compressed air network.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Air Supply Sizing Factors</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Conveying rate and duty cycle:</strong> continuous or batch, and peak rate.</li>
<li><strong>Material properties:</strong> bulk density, particle size and shape, abrasiveness, friability, moisture, and temperature.</li>
<li><strong>Route:</strong> horizontal length, vertical lift, and number of bends. Each bend and each foot of lift adds pressure drop.</li>
<li><strong>Pipe diameter:</strong> required airflow rises with pipe cross-section at a given velocity.</li>
<li><strong>Site conditions:</strong> altitude and inlet air temperature change air density and the airflow a given machine delivers.</li>
</ul>
<p>Ask suppliers to state the design airflow and pressure, the margin included, and whether the figures come from testing your material or from a comparable one.</p>
<h3>Plant Air vs. Dedicated Compressor</h3>
<p>Plant compressed air is convenient for small dense phase transporters, but a conveying cycle can draw down header pressure and starve other equipment. Confirm plant air capacity at peak demand, or budget for a dedicated compressor and receiver tank. Air quality matters too: hygroscopic materials such as sugar and salt absorb moisture and cake in the line, so the air needs drying, and food and pharmaceutical products usually need oil-free compressors or suitable filtration. Generating air at the 45 to 60 psig that dense phase often needs takes significant energy, and the <a href="https://www.energy.gov/eere/iedo/compressed-air-systems" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">U.S. Department of Energy compressed air systems program</a> publishes tools and tip sheets for cutting compressed air energy use.</p>
<h2 id="components-heading">System Components</h2>
<h3>Feed Devices</h3>
<p>Rotary airlock valves meter material from atmospheric-pressure hoppers into pressurized pipelines while holding the pressure seal. They are the standard feed device for dilute phase pressure systems, and air leakage across the rotor must be included in air supply sizing. Pressure vessels batch-feed dense phase lines. Vacuum systems often use suction wands or bag spout adapters with no mechanical feeder.</p>
<figure class="photo"><img src="/img/photos/pneu-flexicon-rotary-airlock-valves.webp" width="860" height="584" alt="Four stainless steel rotary airlock valves in different sizes, each with a flanged round inlet, an electric motor, and a yellow drive guard." loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption>Flexicon rotary airlock valves in four sizes. <span class="photo-credit">Photo: <a href="https://flexicon.com/bulk-handling-equipment-and-systems/pneumatic-conveyors/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Flexicon</a></span></figcaption></figure>
<h3>Pipeline, Bends &amp; Couplings</h3>
<p>Conveying lines use carbon steel, stainless steel, or aluminum pipe or tube. Long-radius swept bends reduce impact compared with short elbows. Tight bends cause product degradation, streamers (angel hair) in plastic pellet systems, and fast elbow wear. Ceramic-lined or replaceable-wear-back elbows extend life in abrasive service. Compression couplings join sections: align them to avoid internal steps that cause wear and buildup, and bond them electrically across joints in combustible dust service.</p>
<figure class="photo"><img src="/img/photos/pneu-flexicon-conveying-lines.webp" width="1000" height="667" alt="Banks of metal conveying pipes with swept bends and bolted couplings run along a brick building beside two rail hopper cars." loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption>Flexicon pneumatic conveying lines with swept bends and couplings, routed along a building beside rail hopper cars. <span class="photo-credit">Photo: <a href="https://flexicon.com/bulk-handling-equipment-and-systems/pneumatic-conveyors/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Flexicon</a></span></figcaption></figure>
<h3>Filter Receivers</h3>
<p>Filter receivers at the destination separate material from transport air. Material drops into the receiving hopper while air passes through cartridges or bags. Media selection depends on particle size, air volume, and emission limits. Pharmaceutical and some food applications add HEPA filtration.</p>
<h3>Diverter Valves</h3>
<p>Diverter valves route material from one line to multiple destinations. A two-way diverter splits at a Y-junction, and multi-port diverters serve several destinations from one source. They remove the need for separate lines and air movers for each destination.</p>
<h2 id="apps-heading">Pneumatic Conveying Applications</h2>
<p><strong>Food ingredient handling:</strong> flour, sugar, starch, spices, and milk powder move from bulk storage to mixing, batching, and packaging equipment, where <a href="/conveyor-functions/infeed-outfeed/">infeed and outfeed conveyors</a> carry the containers being filled. <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/sanitary/">Sanitary designs</a> use stainless steel pipe, quick-disconnect couplings, and cleanable filter receivers. Dense phase protects fragile ingredients such as freeze-dried fruit and snack seasonings.</p>
<p><strong>Plastic resin handling:</strong> dilute phase vacuum systems load pellets from rail cars, silos, and gaylords to molding machines and extruders. Each machine has a vacuum receiver, a central pump powers the system, and a sequencing controller serves each receiver on demand.</p>
<p><strong>Pharmaceutical powders:</strong> contained systems transfer APIs, excipients, and blends between process steps. Dense phase limits attrition and segregation, and cleanable stainless construction prevents cross-contamination.</p>
<p><strong>Cement and minerals:</strong> dense phase pressure systems move cement, fly ash, and mineral powders from silos to process or loadout. Abrasive materials call for low velocity and wear-resistant bends.</p>
<p><strong>Chemical powders:</strong> pigments, catalysts, carbon black, and specialty chemicals. Nitrogen replaces air for oxidation-sensitive or combustible materials.</p>
<p><strong>Bulk bag unloading:</strong> <a href="/conveyor-types/vacuum-conveyors/">vacuum conveying</a> draws material from bulk bag (FIBC) stations through a wand or spout adapter for dust-free transfer to the process hopper.</p>
<figure class="video"><a class="video__link" href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=a9uGGDEBfeo" data-youtube="a9uGGDEBfeo"><img src="/img/photos/yt-a9uggdebfeo.webp" width="1000" height="563" alt="" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><span class="video__play">Play video: Distillery Loads Grain Nine Times Faster with Pneumatic Conveying Upgrade</span></a><figcaption>Flexicon case study of a pneumatic conveying system that moves grain at Dancing Goat Distillery. <span class="photo-credit">Video: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/@flexicon" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Flexicon Corporation</a> on YouTube</span></figcaption></figure>
<h2 id="design-heading">Design Considerations</h2>
<p>For who builds each type of system, see the <a href="/resources/conveyor-manufacturers/pneumatic/">best pneumatic conveying system manufacturers</a> shortlist.</p>
<h3>Energy Consumption</h3>
<p>Maynard notes that pneumatic conveying has the highest specific energy consumption of common conveying methods, because it moves large volumes of air. Dense phase uses less air at higher pressure, which can lower operating cost, but the energy to generate 45 to 60 psig air can still be significant. Dilute phase uses more air at lower pressure. Shorter routes, fewer bends, and an air mover sized to actual throughput rather than theoretical peak keep energy use down.</p>
<h3>Combustible Dust</h3>
<p>Many conveyed materials are combustible dusts (flour, sugar, starch, metal powders, plastic fines). <a href="https://www.nfpa.org/codes-and-standards/nfpa-652-standard-development/652" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NFPA 652</a> (Standard on the Fundamentals of Combustible Dust) requires a Dust Hazard Analysis (DHA) for facilities that handle combustible dust. <a href="https://www.osha.gov/combustible-dust" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">OSHA's combustible dust guidance</a> covers the same hazard. System design must address ignition sources, bonding and grounding, explosion venting or suppression, and inerting for materials with low minimum ignition energy.</p>
<h3>Mechanical vs. Pneumatic Selection</h3>
<p>For short, straight, high-capacity runs, mechanical conveyors (<a href="/conveyor-types/screw-conveyors/">screw</a>, <a href="/conveyor-types/trough-conveyors/">trough belt</a>, drag chain) usually cost less to install and operate. Maynard's selection flowchart points toward mechanical conveying when the requirement exceeds about 50 tons per hour or about 1,500 ft, though specialized pneumatic designs can go beyond those values. Pneumatic conveying wins when the route has multiple elevation changes, wall penetrations, or tight spaces, or when dust containment, product purity, or explosion safety require enclosed transport.</p>
<h3>What to Send Suppliers</h3>
<ul>
<li>Material name, SDS, bulk density, particle size, moisture, and temperature</li>
<li>Required rate, duty cycle (continuous or batch), and future capacity</li>
<li>Layout sketch: horizontal distance, vertical lift, bends, and wall or floor penetrations</li>
<li>Number of pickup points and destinations</li>
<li>Available utilities: plant air capacity and quality, power, space for air movers</li>
<li>Sanitary, containment, and combustible dust requirements, including DHA status</li>
<li>Whether the supplier will test your material before quoting</li>
</ul>
