<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What is a pneumatic conveying system?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>A system that uses air pressure or vacuum to move dry bulk materials through a sealed pipeline. Material travels suspended in a fast airstream (dilute phase) or as slow plugs or a dense bed (dense phase). The enclosed line contains dust, protects product from contamination, and routes around walls, floors, and equipment.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">Dilute phase vs. dense phase?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Dilute (lean) phase runs above the saltation velocity, so particles stay suspended. Dense phase runs below it, so material moves as plugs or a moving bed. Typical figures from AIChE's Chemical Engineering Progress: dilute phase carries less than 15 lb of solids per lb of gas at under 15 psig, and dense phase more than 15 lb per lb at over 30 psig. Dilute phase is usually blower-driven with lower installed cost. Dense phase is usually compressor-driven and gentler on product and pipe.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">Does pneumatic conveying need an air compressor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Not always. Dilute phase pressure systems normally use a positive displacement blower, and vacuum systems use a vacuum pump or exhauster. Dense phase pressure systems need compressed air from a dedicated compressor or the plant network, dried and oil-free where product quality requires it. Confirm plant air can meet peak conveying demand without starving other equipment.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-4">What materials can be pneumatically conveyed?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-4" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Most dry bulk solids: powders (flour, cement, pigments), granules (sugar, salt, coffee), pellets (plastic resin, pet food), flakes (cereal, herbs), and fibers (wood dust). Very wet, sticky, or strongly hygroscopic materials may need special design or suit mechanical conveyors better.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-5">Pneumatic vs. mechanical conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-5" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Pneumatic: enclosed dust-free transport, complex routing, multiple pickup or delivery points, contamination-sensitive or hazardous materials. Pneumatic conveying has the highest specific energy use of common methods. Mechanical: lower energy per ton, and a better fit for high tonnage rates and long distances. Choose pneumatic when containment or routing matters more than energy cost.</p>

</div>
</div>
